Chapter: ExprEvalTest
  cites Dev chapter ExprEval

 The debugger's expression evaluator, one line per case.

 Every case below is a shape that fails differently, and the failures are
 as load-bearing as the successes: an evaluator that answers a number for
 input it did not understand is the defect this whole chapter exists to
 avoid, so roughly half these cases require an error and say which.

 The precedence pair is the one to read first. `1 + 2 * 3` and
 `1 | 2 + 3` differ only in which operators they use, and they pin the
 two-level rule from opposite sides: 7 says multiplication binds tighter,
 and 6 says the or and the plus do not -- `(1 | 2) + 3`, where C would
 give 8 by binding the plus first.

 THE HEX CASES ARE HERE BECAUSE THEY CAUGHT A REAL BUG. `0xff` came back
 as 391 on the first run: the letter-to-value step did `10 + c - 'a'`,
 which is meaningless in CCE, where letters are ordered by English
 frequency and `f` is 28 while `a` is 15. `0xF0 & 0x3C` is the same bug
 seen through an operator, and it answered 64 instead of 48. Both stay,
 in both cases, because a hex scanner that is right for `0x10` and wrong
 for `0xff` passes any test that only uses the first ten digits.

 We say:

Section: Reporting

  probe : Text -> [Console] Nothing
  probe (s) = act
    print-line-uni (s & " => " & expr-show (expr-eval s 4096))
  end

Section: Body

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act

    probe "42"
    probe "0x10"
    probe "0xff"
    probe "0xFF"

    probe "heap-base"
    probe "gop-fb"

    probe "heap-base + 0x100"
    probe "0x10 - 1"
    probe "1 + 2 * 3"
    probe "1 | 2 + 3"
    probe "2 * 3 * 4"
    probe "0xF0 & 0x3C"
    probe "1 << 8"
    probe "0x100 >> 4"
    probe "7 ^ 1"

    probe "$"
    probe "$ + 16"

    probe "heap-base-1"

    probe ""
    probe "0x"
    probe "nosuch"
    probe "1 +"
    probe "1 2"
    probe "0x10 )"
    probe "8 / 0"

  end
